  "Question": "The sides of a right triangle are \\(a,\\) \\(a+d,\\) and \\(a+2d,\\) with \\(a\\) and \\(d\\) both positive. The ratio of \\(a\\) to \\(d\\) is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(1{:}3\\)",
    "B": "\\(1{:}4\\)",
    "C": "\\(2{:}1\\)",
    "D": "\\(3{:}1\\)",
    "E": "\\(3{:}4\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "Because \\(d>0\\), the longest side is \\(a+2d\\), so it must be the hypotenuse. The Pythagorean theorem gives\n\\[\na^2+(a+d)^2=(a+2d)^2.\n\\]\nExpanding the squares and collecting terms yields\n\\[\na^2-2ad-3d^2=0.\n\\]\nFactoring reveals the possible relationships between \\(a\\) and \\(d\\):\n\\[\n(a-3d)(a+d)=0.\n\\]\nSince \\(a\\) and \\(d\\) are positive, \\(a+d>0\\). Therefore \\(a-3d=0\\), so \\(a=3d\\). The ratio is \\(a:d=3:1\\), choice D.",
